{"id":93,"text":"A master potter took on a talented but arrogant apprentice named Lina. She learned quickly, and after a year, she thought she knew everything.\n\n“Your teaching has become unnecessary,” she told her master. “My pots are as good as yours.”\n\nThe master said nothing. He took one of her finest bowls and one of his own and placed them side by side on the table. “Which is better?” he asked.\n\nLina inspected them and, to her surprise, couldn’t decide. They looked almost identical.\n\n“Now,” said the master, “fill both with water.”\n\nShe did so. Her bowl leaked slowly from a tiny crack that she hadn’t noticed. His held every drop.\n\n“The difference between us is invisible to the eye,” said the master. “It’s in the details, in the thousand small decisions you make when nobody is looking. That is not something you can learn in a year. It takes a lifetime.”\n\nLina stared at the puddle. Her pride had blinded her to something simple: being good isn’t the same as being excellent.\n\nShe stayed another ten years. When she finally opened her own workshop, she hung a small mirror above the door. Underneath it, she wrote: “Look carefully before you say you’re finished.”\n\nMoral: Excellence lies in the small details that nobody notices, and humility keeps us learning.","tr":null,"gl":{"a":"bir (noaniq artikl)","master":"usta, ustoz","potter":"kulol","took":"oldi","on":"ustida, -da","talented":"iste'dodli","but":"lekin, ammo","arrogant":"takabbur","apprentice":"shogird","named":"ismli","lina":"Lina (ism)","she":"u (ayol)","learned":"o'rgandi","quickly":"tez","and":"va","after":"keyin, so'ng","year":"yil","thought":"o'yladi; fikr","knew":"bilardi","everything":"hamma narsa","your":"sening, sizning","teaching":"o'qitish","has":"bor","become":"bo'lmoq, aylanmoq","unnecessary":"keraksiz","told":"aytdi","her":"uni; uning (ayol)","my":"mening","pots":"ko'zalar; tuvaklar","are":"-dir, -siz, -lar (to be)","as":"sifatida, kabi, -ganda","good":"yaxshi","yours":"seniki","t